.cpanel.yml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
# Committed build output. Marking it generated keeps a bundle change from drowning review;
# the CI reproducibility gate is what actually establishes trust in it.
"JobMatch Scraper/static/dist/**" linguist-generated=true -diff

# Docs. THE eol=lf IS LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness: build_docs.py --check compares the committed
# bytes against a fresh render, and core.autocrlf=true would give it CRLF on this machine and LF
# on the Linux runner. Without this the gate fails in CI forever while passing locally.
#
# The whole directory, not a list of the generated files. An earlier version named only INDEX.md,
# MAP.md, map.html and img/*.svg, and missed ARCHITECTURE.md -- which is hand-written prose but
# STILL WRITTEN by the generator, because the mermaid blocks are injected between markers. The
# symptom was `git status` reporting it modified while `git diff` showed nothing at all.
"JobMatch Scraper/docs/**" text eol=lf

# Marked generated so a full regeneration does not drown review, same as static/dist. Only the
# files that are generated end to end -- ARCHITECTURE.md is mostly prose and should diff.
"JobMatch Scraper/docs/INDEX.md" linguist-generated=true
"JobMatch Scraper/docs/MAP.md" linguist-generated=true -diff
"JobMatch Scraper/docs/map.html" linguist-generated=true -diff
"JobMatch Scraper/docs/img/*.svg" linguist-generated=true -diff
# Harvested brand logos. THE -text IS LOAD-BEARING, for the same reason as the docs eol=lf
# above and it bites harder: scripts/build_logos.py --check compares every committed file
# against the sha256 recorded in logo_harvest.json, and core.autocrlf=true would rewrite the
# line endings of every .svg on checkout -- so all ~750 of them would fail their own gate on a
# fresh clone and on the deploy host, while passing on the machine that harvested them.
"JobMatch Scraper/static/logos/**" -text linguist-generated=true -diff

# The harvest ledger and the Wikidata class cache: generated, large, and reviewed by their
# summary rather than line by line.
# eol=lf for the same reason the docs need it: the script writes "
", so without this
# core.autocrlf hands it back CRLF and the next run reports the whole file rewritten.
"JobMatch Scraper/logo_harvest.json" text eol=lf linguist-generated=true -diff
"JobMatch Scraper/wikidata_classes.json" text eol=lf linguist-generated=true -diff

# Shell scripts. THE eol=lf IS LOAD-BEARING, and there was no rule here at all until
# 2026-08-25. core.autocrlf=true gave bin/cron_scrape.sh a CRLF working copy, and a CRLF shell
# script on Linux fails on a stray carriage return before it executes a single line. Until this
# rule existed, every hand-deploy of that file had to be routed through `git show HEAD:...` to
# get usable bytes -- a trap that stays disarmed only while whoever is deploying remembers it.
*.sh text eol=lf
